      "text": "We start by expressing the given condition mathematically. We know that the last two digits of \\(11 \\cdot t\\) are 36. This can be written as:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(t\\), we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. The multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is a number \\(x\\) such that:\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nWe can find this inverse using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. We start by applying the Euclidean Algorithm to 11 and 100:\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we can express 1 as:\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\n\nThis equation shows that:\n\\[\n11 \\cdot (-9) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since we need a positive integer, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\n\nSo, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now, we multiply both sides of the original congruence \\(11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\\) by 91:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(36 \\cdot 91\\):\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the last two digits of 3276 by taking \\(3276 \\mod 100\\):\n\\[\n3276 \\mod 100 = 76\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\\). Since \\(t\\) is a two-digit positive integer, the only solution is:\n\\[\nt = 76\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the unique two-digit positive integer \\(t\\) for which the last two digits of \\(11t\\) are 36 is \\(\\boxed{76}\\).",
