      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the equation:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nThis means we need to find \\( t \\) such that:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nTo solve this congruence, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. The multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nWe can use the Euclidean algorithm to find this inverse. First, we apply the Euclidean algorithm to 100 and 11:\n\\[ 100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1, \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0. \\]\nFrom the first equation, we can see that:\n\\[ 1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11. \\]\nThis tells us that:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot (-9) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}, \\]\nso the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since we want a positive inverse, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent modulo 100:\n\\[ -9 \\equiv 91 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can multiply both sides of the original congruence \\( 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\) by 91:\n\\[ t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nNext, we calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[ 36 \\cdot 91 = 3276. \\]\nWe need to find the last two digits of 3276, which is the same as \\( 3276 \\mod 100 \\):\n\\[ 3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nTherefore, \\( t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\). The only two-digit integer that satisfies this congruence is 76. Thus, the value of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{76}. \\]",
