      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find a unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are \\( 36 \\). This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nWe need to find the values of \\( t \\) that satisfy this congruence equation. First, we will rewrite the congruence equation:\n\\[\n11t = 100k + 36 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{100k + 36}{11}\n\\]\nFor \\( t \\) to be an integer, \\( 100k + 36 \\) must be divisible by 11. Therefore, we need to find \\( k \\) such that:\n\\[\n100k + 36 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\nFirst, we simplify \\( 100 \\mod 11 \\):\n\\[\n100 \\div 11 = 9 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 100 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\nThus, the congruence equation becomes:\n\\[\n1 \\cdot k + 36 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nk + 36 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv -36 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\nNext, we simplify \\(-36 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[\n-36 \\div 11 = -3 \\text{ remainder } -3 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -36 \\equiv -3 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\nThis means:\n\\[\nk = 11m + 8 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( k = 11m + 8 \\) back into the expression for \\( t \\):\n\\[\nt = \\frac{100(11m + 8) + 36}{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{1100m + 800 + 36}{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{1100m + 836}{11}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = 100m + 76\n\\]\nWe need \\( t \\) to be a two-digit number, so:\n\\[\n76 \\leq 100m + 76 \\leq 99\n\\]\nSubtracting 76 from all parts of the inequality:\n\\[\n0 \\leq 100m \\leq 23\n\\]\n\\[\n0 \\leq m \\leq 0.23\n\\]\nThe only integer value for \\( m \\) in this range is \\( m = 0 \\). Substituting \\( m = 0 \\) back into the expression for \\( t \\):\n\\[\nt = 100 \\cdot 0 + 76 = 76\n\\]\nThus, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
