      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $t$ be a two-digit positive integer. We can represent $t$ as $10a + b$, where $a$ is the tens digit and $b$ is the ones digit. We are given that the last two digits of $11t$ are $36$, which means that $11t$ ends in $36$. \n\nSo, we can write the equation:\n\\[11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nWe can simplify this equation by dividing both sides by 11:\n\\[t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 10^{-1} \\pmod{10}\\]\n\nTo find the modular inverse of 11 modulo 100, we can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. We have:\n\\[11 = 1 \\cdot 10 + 1\\]\n\\[10 = 10 \\cdot 1 + 0\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\\[1 = 11 - 1 \\cdot 10\\]\n\nTaking this equation modulo 10, we get:\n\\[1 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{10}\\]\n\nSo, the modular inverse of 11 modulo 100 is $-1$, which is equivalent to $99$ modulo 100.\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 99 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nCalculating $36 \\cdot 99$ gives us:\n\\[36 \\cdot 99 = 3564\\]\n\nTaking this modulo 100, we get:\n\\[3564 \\equiv 64 \\pmod{100}\\]\n\nSo, $t \\equiv 64 \\pmod{100}$. Since $t$ is a two-digit positive integer, we have:\n\\[t = 64\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $t$ is $\\boxed{64}$.",
